# Reference (correct) + mutant (one subtle labeled defect) for prompts_nonverifiable.jsonl, keyed by id.
# The mutant matches the reference in length/fluency/confidence — only substance differs (anti-length-bias, findings Era 7).
{"id": "explain-altitude-boiling", "reference": "At higher altitudes the atmospheric pressure pressing on the water's surface is lower. Boiling occurs when a liquid's vapor pressure equals the surrounding atmospheric pressure, so when that surrounding pressure is reduced, the water reaches this balance point at a lower temperature. As a result water may boil at around 90 C on a high mountain rather than 100 C at sea level, which is why cooking takes longer there.", "mutant": "At higher altitudes the atmospheric pressure pressing on the water's surface is lower. Because the thinner air holds far less oxygen, there are fewer oxygen molecules available to sustain vaporization, so the water reaches its boiling point with less added heat. As a result water may boil at around 90 C on a high mountain rather than 100 C at sea level, which is why cooking takes longer there.", "defect": "wrong-fact", "flaw": "It attributes the lower boiling point to reduced oxygen sustaining vaporization, but oxygen plays no role — boiling depends only on vapor pressure equaling atmospheric pressure."}
{"id": "explain-rainbow-colors", "reference": "Sunlight entering a raindrop is refracted (bent), reflected off the back inner surface of the drop, and refracted again as it exits. Because different wavelengths bend by slightly different amounts (dispersion), the white light spreads into a spectrum. Longer-wavelength red light bends the least and appears on the outer (upper) edge of the arc, while shorter-wavelength violet bends the most and appears on the inner edge.", "mutant": "Sunlight entering a raindrop is refracted (bent), reflected off the back inner surface of the drop, and refracted again as it exits. Because different wavelengths bend by slightly different amounts (dispersion), the white light spreads into a spectrum. Shorter-wavelength violet light bends the least and appears on the outer (upper) edge of the arc, while longer-wavelength red bends the most and appears on the inner edge.", "defect": "wrong-fact", "flaw": "It inverts the dispersion and color order: red bends least and sits on the outer edge, violet bends most on the inner edge — the mutant swaps both."}
{"id": "explain-blue-sky", "reference": "Sunlight contains all visible colors, and as it passes through the atmosphere it strikes gas molecules that scatter it. This scattering (Rayleigh scattering) is much stronger for shorter wavelengths, so blue light is scattered far more than red. Because this scattered blue light reaches our eyes from all directions across the sky, the sky looks blue (we see blue rather than violet partly because our eyes are less sensitive to violet).", "mutant": "Sunlight contains all visible colors, and as it passes through the atmosphere it strikes gas molecules. These molecules absorb the longer red and orange wavelengths and let the shorter blue wavelengths pass through to our eyes. Because this transmitted blue light reaches us from all directions across the sky, the sky looks blue (we see blue rather than violet partly because our eyes are less sensitive to violet).", "defect": "conflation", "flaw": "It replaces wavelength-dependent scattering with selective absorption of red light; the sky is blue because short wavelengths are scattered more, not because red is absorbed."}
{"id": "explain-mrna-vaccine", "reference": "An mRNA vaccine delivers a strand of messenger RNA that codes for a specific viral protein, typically the spike protein. Cells take up the mRNA and their ribosomes in the cytoplasm translate it into that protein, which is then displayed to the immune system. The immune system recognizes the protein as foreign and produces antibodies and memory cells, so a later encounter with the real virus prompts a fast response. The mRNA itself is short-lived and is broken down by the cell after use.", "mutant": "An mRNA vaccine delivers a strand of messenger RNA that codes for a specific viral protein, typically the spike protein. The mRNA travels into the cell's nucleus, where it is integrated into the cell's DNA so that the protein can be produced and displayed to the immune system. The immune system recognizes the protein as foreign and produces antibodies and memory cells, so a later encounter with the real virus prompts a fast response. The mRNA itself is short-lived and is broken down by the cell after use.", "defect": "wrong-fact", "flaw": "It claims the mRNA enters the nucleus and integrates into DNA; in reality it stays in the cytoplasm, is translated by ribosomes, and never enters the nucleus or alters DNA."}
{"id": "explain-greenhouse-effect", "reference": "Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide and water vapor are largely transparent to incoming shortwave sunlight, so most of it passes through and warms the surface. The warmed surface then radiates energy back upward as longer-wavelength infrared. Greenhouse gases absorb much of this outgoing infrared and re-emit it in all directions, including back down toward the surface, which keeps the lower atmosphere and surface warmer than they would otherwise be.", "mutant": "Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide and water vapor form a layer in the atmosphere that reflects a large share of incoming shortwave sunlight back down toward the surface, concentrating solar energy near the ground. As more of this reflected sunlight becomes trapped beneath the gas layer, the lower atmosphere and surface grow steadily warmer than they would otherwise be. The effect strengthens as the concentration of these gases rises.", "defect": "inverted-causation", "flaw": "It says greenhouse gases warm the surface by reflecting incoming sunlight downward; they are actually transparent to incoming shortwave light and warm the surface by absorbing and re-emitting outgoing longwave infrared."}
{"id": "explain-sailing-upwind", "reference": "When sailing upwind the sail is trimmed at an angle so that wind flows across it like air over an airfoil, generating an aerodynamic force largely perpendicular to the sail (lift) rather than just a downwind push. The boat's keel or centerboard resists sideways motion through the water, so the sideways part of that force is largely blocked and the remaining component drives the boat forward. By zig-zagging (tacking) the boat makes net progress toward the wind, though it can never sail directly into it.", "mutant": "When sailing upwind the sail is trimmed at an angle so that the wind pushes against its surface, and this direct push is what propels the boat. The boat's keel or centerboard resists sideways motion through the water, so the sideways part of that push is largely blocked and the remaining component drives the boat forward. By zig-zagging (tacking) the boat makes net progress toward the wind, though it can never sail directly into it.", "defect": "flawed-reasoning", "flaw": "It attributes upwind propulsion to the wind pushing (drag) on the sail; upwind sailing relies on aerodynamic lift from airflow across the sail acting as an airfoil, which pure downwind push cannot achieve."}
{"id": "factual-fastest-land-animal", "reference": "The cheetah is the fastest land animal. In short bursts it can reach a top speed of roughly 100 to 120 km/h (about 60 to 75 mph), though it can only sustain such a sprint for a few hundred meters before it risks overheating.", "mutant": "The cheetah is the fastest land animal. In short bursts it can reach a top speed of roughly 70 to 80 km/h (about 45 to 50 mph), though it can only sustain such a sprint for a few hundred meters before it risks overheating.", "defect": "wrong-quantity", "flaw": "It understates the cheetah's top speed, which is about 100 to 120 km/h, not 70 to 80 km/h."}
{"id": "factual-adult-bone-count", "reference": "An adult human body has 206 bones. A newborn actually has more — roughly 270 to 300 — because many of these bones are separate at birth and gradually fuse together as the child grows, reducing the total to 206 by adulthood.", "mutant": "An adult human body has 206 bones. A newborn actually has fewer — roughly 150 to 180 — because additional bones form and ossify as the child grows, increasing the total to 206 by adulthood.", "defect": "inverted-causation", "flaw": "It inverts the developmental change: babies are born with more bones (about 270 to 300) that fuse over time, not fewer bones that increase."}
{"id": "factual-deepest-ocean", "reference": "The deepest known point is the Challenger Deep, at the southern end of the Mariana Trench in the western Pacific Ocean. It reaches a depth of roughly 10,900 to 11,000 meters (about 36,000 feet) below sea level.", "mutant": "The deepest known point is the Challenger Deep, at the southern end of the Mariana Trench in the western Pacific Ocean. It reaches a depth of roughly 8,800 to 8,900 meters (about 29,000 feet) below sea level.", "defect": "wrong-quantity", "flaw": "It gives a depth of about 8,850 meters (roughly Everest's height), far short of the Challenger Deep's true depth of about 10,900 to 11,000 meters."}
{"id": "factual-olympus-mons", "reference": "The tallest known mountain is Olympus Mons, a giant shield volcano on Mars. It stands roughly 22 kilometers high (about 72,000 feet), which is around two and a half times the height of Mount Everest measured from sea level.", "mutant": "The tallest known mountain is Olympus Mons, a giant shield volcano on Mars. It stands roughly 13 kilometers high (about 43,000 feet), which is around one and a half times the height of Mount Everest measured from sea level.", "defect": "wrong-quantity", "flaw": "It understates Olympus Mons's height, which is about 22 km (roughly 2.5 times Everest), not about 13 km."}
{"id": "factual-speed-of-sound", "reference": "Sound travels through air at roughly 343 meters per second at 20 C (about 767 mph). Its speed depends strongly on the air's temperature — faster in warmer air and slower in colder air — because temperature governs how quickly the gas molecules pass the pressure wave along.", "mutant": "Sound travels through air at roughly 343 meters per second at 20 C (about 767 mph). Its speed depends strongly on the air's pressure — faster when atmospheric pressure is higher and slower when it is lower — because denser, higher-pressure air passes the pressure wave along more quickly.", "defect": "wrong-fact", "flaw": "It claims the speed of sound in air depends on pressure, but for an ideal gas it is essentially independent of pressure and instead varies with temperature."}
{"id": "factual-dna-base-pairs", "reference": "In DNA, adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C). The A-T pair is held together by two hydrogen bonds, while the G-C pair is held by three hydrogen bonds, which makes G-C pairs somewhat more stable.", "mutant": "In DNA, adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C). The A-T pair is held together by three hydrogen bonds, while the G-C pair is held by two hydrogen bonds, which makes A-T pairs somewhat more stable.", "defect": "wrong-quantity", "flaw": "It inverts the hydrogen-bond counts: A-T has two bonds and G-C has three, so G-C is the more stable pair, not A-T."}
{"id": "reason-shortest-of-three", "reference": "From Alice > Bob and Bob > Carol, the tallest-to-shortest order is Alice, Bob, Carol. Carol sits at the bottom of the chain, so Carol is the shortest of the three.", "mutant": "From Alice > Bob and Bob > Carol, Bob sits directly below Alice. Being ranked immediately beneath the tallest person in the group, Bob is the shortest of the three.", "defect": "wrong-conclusion", "flaw": "The mutant names Bob as shortest, ignoring that Carol is below Bob in the ordering."}
{"id": "reason-jacket-discount-tax", "reference": "Apply the 25% discount first: $80 x 0.75 = $60. Then add 10% sales tax on the discounted price: $60 x 1.10 = $66. The customer pays $66.", "mutant": "The 25% discount and the 10% tax combine to a net 15% reduction: $80 x (1 - 0.25 + 0.10) = $80 x 0.85 = $68. The customer pays $68.", "defect": "flawed-reasoning", "flaw": "The mutant treats the sequential discount and tax as additive percentages instead of applying them multiplicatively, yielding $68 instead of $66."}
{"id": "reason-trains-meet", "reference": "By 2:00 pm the first train has traveled 50 miles, leaving 250 of the 300 miles between them. From 2:00 pm both close the gap at 50 + 70 = 120 mph, taking 250/120 = 2.083 hours = 2 hours 5 minutes. They meet at 4:05 pm.", "mutant": "By 2:00 pm the first train has traveled 50 miles, leaving 250 of the 300 miles. Both then close the gap at 50 + 70 = 120 mph, taking 250/120 = 2.083 hours = 2 hours 5 minutes. Adding this to the first train's 1:00 pm start, they meet at 3:05 pm.", "defect": "flawed-reasoning", "flaw": "The mutant adds the 2h5m closing time to the 1:00 pm departure instead of to the 2:00 pm moment its own 250-mile gap was measured from, giving 3:05 pm instead of 4:05 pm."}
{"id": "reason-pipes-tank", "reference": "Per-hour rates: A = 1/4, B = 1/6, drain C = -1/12. Net rate = 1/4 + 1/6 - 1/12 = (3 + 2 - 1)/12 = 4/12 = 1/3 tank per hour. Time to fill = 3 hours.", "mutant": "Combine the two fill pipes: A = 1/4 and B = 1/6, so 1/4 + 1/6 = 3/12 + 2/12 = 5/12 tank per hour. Time to fill = 12/5 = 2.4 hours, or 2 hours 24 minutes.", "defect": "subtle-omission", "flaw": "The mutant omits the drain C entirely, adding only the two fill pipes and reporting 2.4 hours instead of the correct 3 hours."}
{"id": "reason-father-son-age", "reference": "Let the son be s, so the father is 3s. In 12 years: 3s + 12 = 2(s + 12) = 2s + 24. Then 3s - 2s = 24 - 12, so s = 12. The son is 12 now (father 36; in 12 years 48 = 2 x 24, which checks).", "mutant": "Let the son be s, so the father is 3s. In 12 years: 3s + 12 = 2(s + 12) = 2s + 24. Bringing terms together, 3s - 2s = 24 + 12, so s = 36. The son is 36 now.", "defect": "flawed-reasoning", "flaw": "The mutant moves the +12 across the equals sign without flipping its sign (using 24 + 12 instead of 24 - 12), producing s = 36 instead of 12."}
{"id": "reason-two-red-marbles", "reference": "P(both red) = (5/8) x (4/7). The first draw is red with probability 5/8; then 4 reds remain out of 7 marbles, so the second is 4/7. Product = 20/56 = 5/14, about 0.36.", "mutant": "P(both red) = (5/8) x (4/8). The first draw is red with probability 5/8; after removing a red, 4 reds remain, so the second is 4/8. Product = 20/64 = 5/16, about 0.31.", "defect": "wrong-quantity", "flaw": "The mutant keeps the denominator at 8 for the second draw (4/8) despite drawing without replacement, giving 5/16 instead of the correct 5/14."}
{"id": "summarize-lunch-walk-study", "reference": "A small, non-randomized study found that office workers who walked after lunch reported modestly greater afternoon alertness, but the authors caution the effect is preliminary and would need larger controlled trials to confirm.", "mutant": "A study found that office workers who walk after lunch are significantly more alert in the afternoon, confirming that a short post-lunch walk reliably boosts afternoon alertness at work.", "defect": "overstatement", "flaw": "The mutant recasts a modest, unconfirmed, non-randomized observational result as a significant and confirmed reliable effect, contrary to the passage's explicit cautions."}
{"id": "summarize-coral-currents", "reference": "Over a decade of surveys, reefs near strong currents recovered from bleaching faster than those in calmer waters, possibly from cooler water, but were also more storm-damaged, leading the authors to call the water-movement/reef-health link complex rather than simply positive.", "mutant": "Over a decade of surveys, reefs in calmer waters recovered from bleaching faster than those near strong currents, leading the authors to conclude that reduced water movement protects reef health and benefits recovery.", "defect": "unfaithful-inversion", "flaw": "The mutant reverses the key finding (calm-water reefs recovered faster) and the conclusion, whereas the passage says current-adjacent reefs recovered faster and calls the relationship complex."}
{"id": "summarize-hybrid-work", "reference": "After adopting a three-day-a-week hybrid schedule, the company found project deadlines were met at roughly the same rate as before, while employees valued the flexibility and reported lower commuting stress.", "mutant": "After adopting a three-day-a-week hybrid schedule, the company found project deadlines were met at roughly the same rate as before, employees valued the flexibility with lower commuting stress, and overall company revenue rose as a result.", "defect": "unfaithful-addition", "flaw": "The mutant adds that revenue rose, a claim the passage explicitly says was not measured."}
{"id": "summarize-sleep-memory", "reference": "In a small lab experiment with healthy young adults, well-rested volunteers recalled somewhat more words than sleep-restricted ones, a statistically significant but small effect the authors say is consistent with, though not proof of, sleep aiding memory consolidation and may not generalize to older people.", "mutant": "In a lab experiment with healthy young adults, well-rested volunteers recalled more words than sleep-restricted ones, a statistically significant effect that demonstrates sleep plays a causal role in consolidating memory, though the authors note it may not generalize to older people.", "defect": "overstatement", "flaw": "The mutant states the result demonstrates a causal role for sleep, whereas the authors described it only as consistent with, but not proof of, such a role."}
{"id": "summarize-battery-electrode", "reference": "A new battery electrode retained 90% of its capacity after 1,000 charge cycles in small lab coin cells under controlled temperatures, better than the current standard, but it has not been tested in full-sized batteries or real devices, with commercialization estimated years away.", "mutant": "A new battery electrode retained 90% of its capacity after 1,000 charge cycles in lab coin cells, outperforming the current standard, and having proven itself it is now being built into full-sized batteries expected to reach commercial devices within about a year.", "defect": "unfaithful-inversion", "flaw": "The mutant claims full-sized batteries are now being built and commercialization is about a year away, directly contradicting the passage's statement that no full-sized battery has been built and several years of development remain."}
{"id": "summarize-bilingual-vocab", "reference": "The study found bilingual children reached vocabulary milestones in each single language slightly later than monolingual peers but knew as many words overall when both languages were combined, with no evidence that bilingualism delayed general cognitive development.", "mutant": "The study found bilingual children reached vocabulary milestones in each single language slightly later than monolingual peers and also knew fewer words overall even when both languages were combined, though the author found no evidence of delayed general cognitive development.", "defect": "unfaithful-inversion", "flaw": "The mutant inverts the passage's key point by claiming the bilingual children knew fewer words overall, whereas the source says they knew as many words when both languages were combined."}
{"id": "instruct-formal-reschedule", "reference": "Unfortunately, we are unable to accommodate the meeting on Tuesday, so we would like to reschedule it for next week.", "mutant": "Unfortunately, we are unable to accommodate the meeting on Tuesday, so we would like to reschedule it for later this week.", "defect": "meaning-drift", "flaw": "The rewrite changes the meaning by moving the meeting to 'later this week' when the original clearly says 'next week'."}
{"id": "instruct-kyoto-two-part", "reference": "For mid-November, Kyoto is cool and often crisp, with daytime highs around 15C and chilly evenings, so pack layers, a warm jacket, and comfortable walking shoes; a compact umbrella is wise since brief rain is common. At the temples, dress modestly, remove your shoes where indicated, keep your voice low, avoid flash photography or photos where signs prohibit them, and don't touch statues or artifacts.", "mutant": "For mid-November, Kyoto is cool and often crisp, with daytime highs around 15C that drop noticeably after sunset, so pack layers you can add or remove, a warm but packable jacket, and broken-in walking shoes for the long stone paths. A compact umbrella is also wise, since mid-November brings frequent brief showers, and bring a small daypack because the autumn foliage crowds mean you'll be outdoors for long stretches between sites.", "defect": "partial-answer", "flaw": "Mutant answers only the weather part in depth and omits the requested temple etiquette rules entirely."}
{"id": "instruct-complaint-no-liability", "reference": "Thank you for reaching out, and I'm truly sorry for the frustration and mess this has caused you. I completely understand how disappointing it is when an appliance doesn't perform as you expected so soon after purchase. I'd like to make this right for you right away: we can arrange a free replacement unit or a full refund, whichever you prefer, and we'll cover return shipping for the original blender. Just let me know which option works best and I'll get it processed today.", "mutant": "Thank you for reaching out, and I'm truly sorry for the frustration and mess this has caused you. You're right that this unit was clearly defective and never should have failed and leaked the way it did, and that's entirely on us. I'd like to make this right for you right away: we can arrange a free replacement unit or a full refund, whichever you prefer, and we'll cover return shipping for the original blender. Just let me know which option works best and I'll get it processed today.", "defect": "constraint-violation", "flaw": "Mutant admits the company was at fault ('this unit was clearly defective... that's entirely on us'), violating the instruction to avoid admitting fault or liability."}
{"id": "instruct-feedback-no-attack", "reference": "Anchor the conversation in specific, observable behaviors and their impact rather than judgments about the person: for example, 'The last two reports came in a day late, which held up the client review,' instead of a broad verdict. Describe the gap between current and expected results plainly, then shift quickly to a forward-looking, collaborative tone, asking what obstacles they're facing and agreeing on concrete next steps. Keep the focus on the work and on their capacity to improve, and close by expressing genuine confidence that they can turn things around.", "mutant": "Anchor the conversation in specific, observable behaviors and their impact rather than vague complaints: for example, 'The last two reports came in a day late, which held up the client review.' Be direct that their results are lagging well behind the rest of the team and that this pattern really comes down to a lack of drive and focus on their part. Then shift to a forward-looking, collaborative tone, asking what obstacles they're facing and agreeing on concrete next steps, and close by expressing genuine confidence that they can turn things around.", "defect": "constraint-violation", "flaw": "Mutant attacks the employee's character ('a lack of drive and focus on their part') and compares them unfavorably to peers, exactly the demoralizing, personal language the instruction says to avoid."}
{"id": "instruct-compound-gap-accelerates", "reference": "The key is that with compound interest each year's interest is added to the principal, so the next year you earn interest on that interest too. Simple interest adds the same fixed amount every year, so its extra grows in a straight line. But the compound 'interest-on-interest' portion is calculated on a base that is itself getting larger every year, so the amount added grows a bit more each period than it did the period before. Because those yearly increments themselves keep increasing, the gap doesn't just grow — it grows by larger and larger amounts, which is why it accelerates over time.", "mutant": "The key is that with compound interest each year's interest is added back to the principal, so the next year you earn interest on a larger balance, whereas simple interest only ever earns interest on the original principal. That interest-on-interest effect is why compound interest pulls ahead of simple interest and keeps pulling further ahead as the years go on. Over a long enough horizon this compounding becomes very powerful, which is exactly why advisors stress investing early and letting the balance grow untouched for as many years as possible.", "defect": "off-target", "flaw": "Mutant re-explains why compound interest outpaces simple interest — which the student already understands — rather than why the gap between them widens at an accelerating rate."}
{"id": "instruct-antibiotic-handling", "reference": "Keep the medicine in the fridge at all times so it stays effective, and give it a good shake before every single dose, since it settles and needs to be mixed evenly. Always measure each dose with the syringe that came with it rather than a kitchen spoon, because household spoons aren't accurate and your child could get too much or too little. It's really important to give every dose until the full course is finished, even if your child seems completely better after a few days, so the infection is fully cleared. Finally, throw the bottle away 10 days after the pharmacist mixed it, even if some is left, as it isn't safe to use after that.", "mutant": "Keep the medicine in the fridge at all times so it stays effective, and give it a good shake before every single dose, since it settles and needs to be mixed evenly. Always measure each dose with the syringe that came with it rather than a kitchen spoon, because household spoons aren't accurate and your child could get too much or too little. Try to give the doses at evenly spaced times through the day so the medicine keeps working steadily, and pair a dose with a small snack if it seems to upset your child's stomach. Finally, throw the bottle away 10 days after the pharmacist mixed it, even if some is left, as it isn't safe to use after that.", "defect": "subtle-omission", "flaw": "Mutant omits the instruction to finish the full course even if the child seems better, substituting unrelated timing/snack tips while matching length."}
